it in about two weeks. After that, they'll Ä weeks ―ing and
fixing it.
Most people need about two to three hours a day to сe a
. That's ideal, but sometimes not very practical. In many
of my ª classes, I tell my students that if they're work-
ing full-time and cannot Ä an hour or two a day сing before
they go to work, or when they get home, they need to keep the idea
current in their minds. They ে need to think about the

story line, the ρters, and "what § next." Sometimes I tell
them to carry the cards with them, so they can go over the material
when they're standing in line or riding on the subway, bus, or train.
Keeping a tape recorder with you when you're ि to work, on
】 or coffee break, or on your way home, helps you to focus on
your Ñ and ideas so you can remember them. Then, before
you fall asleep at night, listen to your ideas or lines of £ and
you'll be able to keep the material fresh in your memory. When the
weekend rolls around, you can Ä around two or three quality
hours on Saturday and/or Sunday working on the script.
What's the best time for you to сe? Look at your daily sched-
ule. Examine your time. If you're working full-time, or caring for
home and family, your time is limited. You're going to have to find
the best time for you to сe. Are you the kind of person who
works best in the Á? Or does it take you until early ח
to be wide awake and alert? Late at night may be a good time. You're
going to have to experiment. Find out.
You may get up and сe a few hours before you go to work; or
you might come home from work, unwind, and then сe a few
hours. You may want to work at night, say about 10 or 11 P.M., or
